  "question": {
    "text": "Lassaigne's test is used for detection of:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "IG0Sp",
      "text": "Nitrogen and Sulphur only",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "61WsU",
      "text": "Nitrogen, Sulphur and Phosphorous Only",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "spjNi",
      "text": "Phosphorous and halogens only",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "DH9Ey",
      "text": "Nitrogen, Sulphur, and halogens",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Lassaigne's test helps to detect Nitrogen, Sulphur, and Halogens in organic compounds. Here, sodium metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>Na</mi></mfenced></math> is heated in the presence of the organic compound inside a fusion tube. The primary objective of this test is that all the available elements are converted by Sodium metal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>Na</mi></mfenced></math> into the ionic state. Lassaigne test reaction for sodium is given below:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>NaCN</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>Na</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Na</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>NaX</mi></math></p><p>In the third experiment, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> can be anything such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Br</mi></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
